rustc:     rustc 1.97.1 (8bab26f4f 2026-07-14)
build:     --release --features encode (no -C target-cpu=native, per repo policy)

WHAT IS MEASURED
  Wall time from StopSource::cancel() to zenavif::decode_with() returning.
  40 trials per cell, cancel point swept across the decode (frac = (i+0.5)/40
  * 0.9 of the control decode time) so the sample covers early/middle/late
  cancellation. Trials where the decode finished before the cancel landed are
  discarded (that is the n column; n < 40 means the decode outran late cancel
  points). Source images are high-frequency synthetic patterns encoded at
  speed 10 -- NOT smooth gradients, which compress to nothing and decode too
  fast to interrupt.

  relay=off reproduces the PRE-CHANGE behavior exactly: the caller's token is
  never handed to rav1d-safe, so it is polled only at zenavif's phase
  boundaries and an in-flight frame decode runs to completion.
  relay=on is the shipped behavior: src/cancel.rs relays the borrowed token
  into rav1d-safe's Decoder::set_stop, which polls at superblock-row
  boundaries.

MULTITHREADED CELLS REQUIRE imazen/rav1d-safe#445
  The first version of this sweep could only report threads=1: with
  threads>=2 the 4K decodes FAILED outright, rav1d-safe tile workers panicking
  with "overlapping DisjointMut". That was the strided block guard reserving
  inter-row gaps that neighbouring tiles legitimately write (rav1d-safe#445,
  the zenavif#30 defect class). These threads=4/8 rows were measured with that
  fix applied via the temporary ../rav1d-safe path dep.

VERDICT ON THE ~5 ms GOAL (p50 and p99, relay on)
  met      64x64, 256x256, 1024x1024, 2048x2048  -- at every thread count
           measured (2048x2048 p99 is 8.69 ms at threads=1 and 5.16 ms at
           threads=4, so the p99 claim at that size needs threads>=4)
  NOT met  3840x2160 -- p50 6.4-7.0 ms, p99 11.9-14.6 ms

  The 4K residual is NOT this crate's poll interval (1 ms). It is rav1d-safe's
  own check spacing of one superblock row. Threading does not remove it: the
  control decode only falls 476 -> 348 ms from 1 to 8 threads because the test
  bitstream carries few tiles, so there is little tile parallelism to exploit,
  and zenavif pins max_frame_delay=1 (no frame threading). Getting 4K under
  5 ms needs a finer check inside rav1d-safe, or a tiled bitstream.
